In article <cpckpu$bmt$1@news2.zwoll1.ov.home.nl>, Boudewijn Waijers
<kroisos@REMOVETHISWORD.home.nl> wrote:
> Rast wrote:
> 
> > Check.  We can also include accomplishments not necessary for
> > ascension, like completing Sokoban and fetching the luckstone.
> 
> These accomplishments make ascension easier and more likely, so they
> already carry a reward in itself.

Right; I thought of including them in the list, but decided against for
that reason.  I don't think anyone should feel obliged to complete
Sokoban just for the sake of completing it.


> > That's an interesting idea.  If Wizard One beats the Dark One at turn
> > 10k, and then dies at the Castle, should his score be higher than
> > Wizard Two, who beat the Dark One at turn 20k and then died at the
> > Castle?  Opinions may vary on this one, too.
> 
> Especially if dying at the Castle happened on turn 25k for both wizards.
> Why should beating the Dark One sooner be rewarded more when you're
> going to stall later?

Regardless of how much you stall later, you still beat the Dark One
with alacrity and style.  And if you don't die at the Castle, stalling
will cost you bonus points at the next step you complete.


> > I would simply give a bonus for each character level from 2-13, and a
> > big bonus for level 14.  Small bonuses could be awarded for reaching
> > lvls 20, 25, 30; those aren't needed, but they do make your character
> > more powerful, so they count as progress towards ascension.

I like the more granular approach of tying the bonus to EXP--makes it
less likely that scores will tie.


> >> - Carnage score--points awarded for each monster killed.
> > I think this is already well-covered by giving points for experience
> > levels gained.
> 
> Not really. One of the reasons for changing the scoring system should
> be, in my opinion, to remove the link between scoring and experience.
> For example, I think that your experience should go up by the same
> amount for each monster of a certain type you killed, while I believe
> that your score should go up by a steadily decreasing amount for each
> monster of the same type killed.
> 
> Hence, Carnage score is, in my opinion, a good thing, since you may make
> it less for repeating the same kills. Of course, that would mean that
> experience should no longer give score (or maybe only the landmarks:
> changing EXP level).

Good point; if score comes directly from kills, the EXP score could be
discarded entirely.

I really like the idea of awarding points for diversity of kills--your
200th black pudding shouldn't give you more than a point or two of
score.

Possibly a bonus for extinctions?  Although I wouldn't want to force
everyone trying for a high score to go for an extinctionist
ascention...


> >> - Wealth--points awarded for zorkmids of wealth owned at the end of
> >> the game, decreasing exponentially with higher numbers of zorkmids.
> > Again, why?
> 
> I can think of a reason: making choices. Do you want to spend lots of
> money on priests, gaining protection and other benefits, or would you
> rather keep your money, for a direct effect on your score? This could be
> a difficult choice, if you're playing for score.

It also gives gems a purpose.  (Is wealth at ascention currently
figured in to score?  My memory says yes, but I'm not positive.)

The decay in bonus-per-zorkmid at higher numbers of zorkmids is to
discourage people from farming near-infinite quantities of wealth
before ascending, of course.


> > The problem is that the point awards you've suggested are decent for
> > comparing two non-ascensions, but horrible for comparing two
> > ascensions.  And we'd still have the problem that sometimes winning
> > the game gives a lower score than losing the game.
> 
> There should be two high score lists: one for ascensions and one for
> non-ascensions. Score calculations may differ for the two.

I'd keep them the same.

I think that allowing a very skillful non-ascention to have a higher
score than a mediocre ascention is a positive feature.

